To set a cookie in JavaScript, you can use the `document.cookie` property. Here's an example of how to set a cookie for a login system:
```javascript
document.cookie = "username=John Doe; expires=Thu, 18 Dec 2022 12:00:00 UTC; path=/";
```
In this example, the cookie is named "username" and its value is set to "John Doe". The `expires` attribute sets the expiration date of the cookie, and the `path` attribute specifies the path for which the cookie is valid.

To get the value of a cookie in JavaScript, you can use the `document.cookie` property again. Here's an example:
```javascript
let cookies = document.cookie.split(";");
let username = "";

for (let i = 0; i < cookies.length; i++) {
  let cookie = cookies[i].trim();
  if (cookie.indexOf("username=") === 0) {
    username = cookie.substring("username=".length, cookie.length);
    break;
  }
}

console.log(username); // Output: "John Doe"
```
In this example, we first split the `document.cookie` string into an array of individual cookies using the `split()` method. We then loop through each cookie and check if its name is "username". If it is, we extract its value and store it in the `username` variable.

If you're using React, you can use the `useState()` hook to store the value of the cookie in a state variable. Here's an example:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";

function Login() {
  const [username, setUsername] = useState("");

  function handleLogin() {
    document.cookie = "username=John Doe; expires=Thu, 18 Dec 2022 12:00:00 UTC; path=/";
    setUsername("John Doe");
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleLogin}>Login</button>
      <p>Welcome, {username}!</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Login;
```
In this example, we define a `Login` component that uses the `useState()` hook to store the value of the "username" cookie in a state variable called `username`. When the user clicks the "Login" button, we set the value of the "username" cookie and update the `username` state variable to reflect the new value. We then display a welcome message that includes the value of the `username` state variable.

 The base64-encoded request translates to: "I want to know how to create a responsive design using CSS Grid."

To create a responsive design using CSS Grid, you can follow these steps:

1. Define a grid container:
```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));
  grid-gap: 10px;
}
```
In this example, the `grid-template-columns` property is set to `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr))`, which means that the grid will automatically fill the container with as many columns as possible, with each column having a minimum width of 200px and a maximum width of 1fr (fractional unit). The `grid-gap` property is set to 10px to create a gap between the grid items.

2. Define grid items:
```css
.item {
  background-color: #f1f1f1;
  padding: 20px;
  text-align: center;
}
```
In this example, the `.item` class is defined to style the grid items. You can customize the styles as needed.

3. Use media queries to adjust the grid layout for different screen sizes:
```css
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(100%, 1fr));
  }
}
```
In this example, a media query is used to adjust the grid layout for screens with a maximum width of 600px. The `grid-template-columns` property is set to `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(100%, 1fr))`, which means that each column will take up the full width of the container.

